@node Differential Examples
@section Example applications

The operators of the previous sections combine into real applications.
libdtfl ships the differential graph and sequence algorithms built from
them, in @code{dtfl/algo.h}.

@subsection Graph computation

A graph is a collection of edge records; a graph algorithm is a
differential program over it, so the result is maintained as edges are
added and removed.  @code{dtfl_algo_bfs} labels each node with its
breadth-first distance from a set of roots: it seeds the roots at
distance zero and iterates, joining each labelled node against outgoing
edges to reach the destination at distance plus one, concatenating the
roots back in, and reducing to keep the minimum distance per node.
@code{dtfl_algo_scc} keeps only the edges inside a strongly connected
component; @code{dtfl_algo_propagate} assigns each node the minimum
label reachable along directed edges.  Each takes collections of
@code{u64} node records and @code{(u64, u64)} edge records with the
signed default diff class, and returns a new collection
(@pxref{API Reference}).

@subsection Interactive queries

Because a differential computation is maintained, not recomputed, it
serves interactive queries cheaply: build and maintain a large changing
collection once, and let many later queries read it.  The mechanism is a
shared arrangement whose trace outlives the dataflow that built it
(@pxref{Arrangements}); a query imports the trace into its own dataflow
and reads it without re-indexing the data.

@subsection Real-time streaming input

Real-time input is the same input session driven by events as they
arrive: insert and remove records as the world changes, advance the
epoch to mark a batch complete, flush, and step.  The frontier a probe
reports tells the program which output is final as of which time, so a
consumer can act on settled results while later times are still in
flight.
